course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 20
title: Head, hands, feet and the rest of the body
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: English cuts the body into more pieces than Ukrainian does: where English has "arm" and "hand", Ukrainian has one word, ruka; where English has "leg" and "foot", one word, nOha; and pAlets is both a finger and a toe.
explain: So when Sashko says "bolyt nOha", his leg or his foot hurts — you have to look, or ask "de?", where?
explain: The head is holovA ("bolyt holovA" is a headache), the eye Oko, two eyes OchI, the ear vukhO, the nose nis — which sounds just like English "niece".
explain: The tummy is zhyvIt, and at a sleepover his mum will remind you to "chystyty zUby", brush your teeth.
explain: One phrase to know but not to use: "zakryi rota", shut your mouth — it is rude, and a parent will tell you off for it.
fact: "holovA" means head, and "bolyt holovA" means "I have a headache".
fact: "ruka" means both arm and hand; "daI ruku" means "give me your hand".
fact: "nOha" means both leg and foot, so "bolyt nOha" can mean the leg or the foot hurts.
fact: "Oko" means eye, and "OchI" means eyes.
fact: "vukhO" means ear, and "vUkha" means ears.
fact: "nis" means nose and sounds like the English word "niece".
fact: "rot" means mouth; "zakryi rota" means "shut your mouth", which is rude, and a parent will scold a child for it.
fact: "zUby" means teeth, and "chystyty zUby" means to brush your teeth.
fact: "zhyvIt" means tummy or belly; "bolyt zhyvIt" is a tummy ache, and kids also say "puzo" or "puzyko", cheeky but fine among friends.
fact: "spynA" means back, the back of your body.
fact: "pAlets" means a finger or a toe, and "pAltsi" means fingers or toes; one word does for both.
fact: "volOssya" means hair, and "kosy" means plaits.
exercise: Sashko says "bolyt holovA". What hurts? | His head | His tummy | His back | His knee
exercise: Sashko's mum says "daI ruku" before crossing the road. What does she want? | Your hand | Your school bag | Your phone | Your bus ticket
exercise: What can "ruka" mean? | Arm or hand | Only the arm | Only the hand | The shoulder
exercise: Sashko limps: "bolyt nOha". What could hurt? | His leg or his foot | Only his foot, not his leg | Only his knee | His hip
exercise: Sashko's granny says his little sister has blue "OchI". What does she mean? | Eyes | Ears | Socks | Shoes
exercise: What is "Oko"? | One eye | Two eyes | An ear | An eyebrow
exercise: What are "vUkha"? | Ears | Eyes | Cheeks | Lips
exercise: Which Ukrainian body word sounds like the English word "niece"? | nis, the nose | rot, the mouth | zUby, the teeth | spynA, the back
exercise: A big kid snaps "zakryi rota!" at Sashko. What did he say? | Shut your mouth, which is rude | Open your mouth, please | Brush your teeth | Close the door
exercise: At a sleepover Sashko's mum says "chystyty zUby!". What should you do? | Brush your teeth | Wash your hands | Go to sleep | Put your pyjamas on
exercise: Sashko holds his belly: "bolyt zhyvIt". What has he got? | A tummy ache | A bad headache | Toothache | Backache
exercise: Sashko's mate pats his "puzo" and laughs. What is he patting? | His belly, a cheeky word for it | His pocket | His back | His head
exercise: Sashko's grandad says "bolyt spynA" after gardening. What hurts? | His back | His bottom | His neck | His feet
exercise: Sashko counts on his "pAltsi". What is he using? | His fingers | His teeth | His toes only | His pencils
exercise: Sashko's sister has long "volOssya" in "kosy". What do you see? | Long hair in plaits | Long arms in sleeves | Long socks in shoes | A long scarf in knots
test: my mate says Ukrainian must have different words for finger and toe, like English. right? | No, pAlets means both | Yes, pAlets is only a finger | Yes, but nobody knows the toe word | No, they have no word for toes
test: Sashko yells "ow, nOha!" after kicking the ball barefoot. What hurts most likely? | His foot | His arm | His head | His hand
test: Your little brother says "zakryi rota" to Sashko's mum as a joke. What happens? | She is shocked and tells him off, because it is rude | She laughs, it means "how are you" | She thanks him | Nothing, it means "close the door"
test: "nis" sounds like "niece". does it mean niece then? | No, it means nose | Yes, it means niece | Yes, it means cousin | No, it means knees
test: In a cartoon a boy asks for "ruka" and gets only a glove for his hand, but he wanted a sleeve too. What did the cartoon forget? | ruka means arm as well as hand | ruka means only a foot | ruka means a glove | ruka means a sleeve
test: Sashko's mum asks "de bolyt?" and he points at his tummy. Which word does he say? | zhyvIt | spynA | holovA | volOssya
test: Your friend thinks "OchI" is one eye and "Oko" is two. Which is right? | Oko is one eye, OchI is two | Both mean one eye, it's the same | Both mean two eyes | Oko means ear
test: Sashko giggles "puzyko" at his baby brother. Is it a rude swear word? | No, a cheeky but fine word for belly | Yes, a very rude word | Yes, never say it | No, it means teeth
test: Sashko's dad lifts a heavy box and groans "oi, spynA!". What hurts? | His back | His finger | His ear | His tooth
test: What would happen if you asked Sashko's sister to hold out her "ruku"? | She'd hold out her hand, arm and all | She'd show you her foot | She'd point at her nose | She'd open her mouth
test: At the dentist Sashko's sister is told to open her "rot" and show her "zUby". What does she show? | Her teeth in her mouth | Her ears and her little nose | Her hands and fingers | Her hair and plaits
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the scope of the Ukrainian noun "ruka"? | It designates both the arm and the hand | It designates the hand exclusively | It designates the arm exclusively | It designates the leg and the foot
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression "zakryi rota" and its standing in polite society. | "Shut your mouth"; it is regarded as rude | "Open your mouth"; it is regarded as courteous | "Brush your teeth"; it is regarded as neutral | "Close the door"; it is regarded as formal
exam: It is given that a child reports "bolyt nOha". Identify the region of the body to which the complaint may pertain. | The leg or the foot | The arm or the hand | The head or the neck | The abdomen exclusively
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the noun "pAlets". | It designates either a finger or a toe | It designates a finger exclusively | It designates the palm of the hand | It designates the knee
